4. Anti-recursion proof — the fix must not reintroduce the class
4.1 The requirement
Every proper fix must include:

Failure class identification — which class(es) does this fix address?

Class regression test — does the fix reintroduce any class previously fixed? Prove not.

Class taxonomy lookup — has this class appeared before? If so, why did it recur?

Proof of non-reintroduction — test, mutation test, or adversarial probe.

4.2 The class taxonomy
The estate maintains a living taxonomy of failure classes — every class ever fixed is registered. Every new fix is checked against it.

Field	Purpose
Class ID	Stable identifier
Definition	What makes something this class
Historical instances	Every prior occurrence
Prior fixes	What was tried, what worked
Regression tests	Proof the class is blocked
Recurrence count	How often it has returned
Root-cause status	Fixed systemically / still at risk
4.3 The rule
A fix cannot be marked "done" unless it proves it does not reintroduce any class in the taxonomy — or explicitly acknowledges the trade and justifies it as a loan.

4.4 Why this sets us apart
The industry tests fixes against the target bug. We test fixes against the entire class of every bug ever fixed. This is how you stop chasing your tail — you prove the tail isn't regrowing.

5. Oscillation detection — the loop breaker
5.1 The signal
The same failure class appearing, being fixed, and reappearing = oscillation. The estate must detect it automatically.

5.2 Detector rules
Same class recurs within N days → flag oscillation

Two classes alternate (A→B→A) → flag trade-off oscillation

Loans for the same bottleneck repeat → flag bypass recursion

Debt in a subsystem grows faster than repayment → flag debt spiral

5.3 The circuit breaker
When oscillation is detected:

Stop accepting quick fixes for the affected class — root-cause mode forced.

Escalate to founder with the full loop history.

Open a compounding fix to the meta-process — not the symptom.

Block further loans in the affected area until root cause is addressed.

Post-review to update taxonomy, detector, and protocol.

5.4 Why this is novel
Most systems detect individual failures. We detect the loop itself — and force the system out of it. The circuit breaker is the difference between tail-chasing forever and converging.

